  "repeats": [],
  "notes": [
   "Fix for the validator errors: each breadboard hole takes only one lead. The jumper wire and the resistor had both been placed in row e (8t.e, 17t.e, 25t.e). The resistor stays in row e. The jumper wire now goes in row d of the same column. Rows a-e of a column are one strip, so the circuit is unchanged.",
   "The image's top half of the breadboard is labelled rows j to f, so I mapped it to catalogue bank 'b' (rows f-j). The image's bottom half is rows e to a, which I mapped to bank 't'. The ground rail drawn at the top of the image is therefore named 'bn'.",
   "Rail hole numbers (bn.1, bn.6, bn.13, bn.20) are estimated from the picture. All holes in one rail are a single strip, so the exact numbers don't change the circuit.",
   "Each channel works like this: an Arduino pin (8, 7 or 6) goes by a jumper wire to column 8, 17 or 25. A 100 ohm resistor spans the centre gap from that column's row e to its row f. The speaker's red lead sits in row g of the same column, which is the same strip as row f. The speaker's black lead goes to the ground rail. The speaker is not polarized, so its ports 1 and 2 are interchangeable.",
   "Arduino GND (the pin next to AREF, taken as GND.1) goes by a black jumper wire to the ground rail.",
   "The manual's parts list and circuit text say 100 ohm resistors, but the schematic labels them 10k. I kept 100 ohm, following the parts list.",
   "The parts list is unchanged from step 1."
  ]
 },
